            <ArticleTitle>Association study between the neurexin-1 gene and tardive dyskinesia.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/hup.2568</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Tardive dyskinesia (TD) is a motor side effect that may develop after long-term antipsychotic treatment. Schizophrenia has recently been associated with the Neurexin-1 (NRXN1) gene that codes for a cell adhesion molecule in synaptic communication.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This study examined five NRXN1 single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) for possible association with the occurrence and severity of TD in 178 schizophrenia patients of European ancestry.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">We did not find these SNPs to be significantly associated with TD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">More research is needed with additional SNPs and in bigger samples before we can completely rule out the role of NRXN1 in TD.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2017 John Wiley &amp; Sons, Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
